From 431a01e68f665f1aaeea9d76176a699c17e8d459 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 06:30:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ActiveAE: a device change event over the bound of a second is
 acted on later, not dropped

ActiveAE acts on a device change event by closing its output, listing the
outputs again and opening it again, three times within a second at most:
any more were dropped ("received N device change events within one
second") and never acted on later, so the outputs stayed as the last
listing found them until another event came. A mode set of the display
brings two events (the ELD of the TV goes, and comes back), two mode sets
within a second bring four, and the one dropped can be the one with which
the TV's audio data came back: an HDMI output listed without the TV's name
and with the channels and formats of no display. The second was also one
of the wall clock (time_t), which a step of the clock back kept full for
as long as the step, every event dropped meanwhile.

CDeviceChangePace (ActiveAEDeviceChangePace.h) keeps the bound, over any
second of the steady clock, and keeps an event over it: the outputs are
listed again once, for every event kept, when the oldest listing of that
second is a second old. A one-shot timer sends the wake-up then, as a
device change event marked as such; an event acted on before it covers the
ones kept, and the wake-up then does nothing, so that no listing is made
twice for the same events and a storm of events makes no more listings than
before. kodi.log says when an event is kept, and when the outputs are
listed again for the ones kept.

TestActiveAEDeviceChangePace.cpp runs it on the host: the fourth event
kept and listed once, a wake-up that an event covered, a storm bounded with
its last event listed, and no event of a run left without a listing after
it.
